  <div class="wrap page-body">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Why anonymous</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>buDaoVoid</strong> is a handle, not a mask. The work should be read without a
        résumé attached, and the frame around it should be mine. Anonymity is also a boundary: I
        write about ideas and systems, not about my family, my school, my city, or my health. Those
        stay private. What is published here is selected, dated, and reversible.
      </p>
      <p>
        The name came from a blog I keep under the same handle. The subtitle of both is a single
        sentence: <em>truth is perpendicular to time.</em> Time is the horizontal axis — the feed,
        the sequence of events, the résumé. The line I care about cuts across it at a right angle.
      </p>

      <h2>What I actually do</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Hardware.</strong> I spent four-plus years on a storm-drain insert that senses
        debris and grinds it before a flood. It has a national patent and a novelty report. It also
        has no installed unit, a sensor that fouls, and a solar panel that sits in the shade. The
        gap between the patent and the street is the part I keep working on.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Software.</strong> I build tools for decisions I cannot make on instinct: a fund
        dashboard with more than twenty indicators and a scoring engine I no longer entirely trust;
        a reader that glosses unfamiliar words without replacing the page; a college-selection aid
        that argues with rankings instead of obeying them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Systems.</strong> I keep a private, versioned archive of how I think: a profile, a
        memory directory, agent skills, and an archive of conversations. The public site is the
        part that crosses the line. Most of it does not.
      </p>

      <h2>Operating principles</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Simplify the layers.</strong> Paper has one layer between a hand and a mark. An
          iPad has four. Every layer adds friction, so the first question is which layers carry
          structure and which only carry delay.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Engineer the inertia.</strong> Willpower is an unreliable power source. A system
          that keeps moving when attention fails is worth more than one perfect decision.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Start from the broken part.</strong> Do not begin with the theory of drainage.
          Begin with the drain full of leaves, the worker kneeling in the water, and the
          maintenance schedule nobody read.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Publish the method.</strong> Show the reasoning and the failures, not only the
          result. Open the method; keep the private material private.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Coordinates</h2>
      <p>
        I look at a lot of painting: Edward Hopper, René Magritte, Salvador Dalí, Zdzisław
        Beksiński, Vincent van Gogh. The thread is not style. It is the loneliness of a lit room,
        the ordinary object made strange, the persistence of a surface that refuses to explain
        itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Music: Radiohead, from <em>Pablo Honey</em> through <em>In Rainbows</em>; Frank Ocean's
        <em>Blonde</em>. Texts that stay on the desk: Qian Xuesen's <em>On Systems Engineering</em>,
        Kropotkin's <em>Mutual Aid</em>, Camus's <em>The Myth of Sisyphus</em>.
      </p>

      <h2>Toolchain, and what I did not author</h2>
      <p>
        I build with Claude Code, Astro, TypeScript, Python, Cloudflare, and Arduino. Some tools in
        my daily stack are forks or other people's work: <a href="https://github.com/Panniantong/agent-reach">Agent Reach</a>
        by Panniantong, <a href="https://github.com/virgiliojr94/book-to-skill">Book-to-Skill</a>
        by virgiliojr94, and <a href="https://github.com/asuojun/claude-vision-skill">Claude Vision Skill</a>
        by asuojun. I use and adapt them. I did not author them, and they are not listed as my work.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Use the <a href="/contact/">contact form</a> for a private message, or the
        <a href="/guestbook/">guestbook</a> for something public. There is no newsletter and no
        mailing list. Replies may take a while.
      </p>
